I understand that you want to create a Sankey chart that shows the flow of money from ABC suppliers to XYZ, and then to DEF suppliers, with the twist that some of the money is " beingcut" or reduced, and you want to visualize this reduction as a link in the Sankey chart.

To achieve this, you can use a combination of D3.js and Sankey.js libraries. Here's a high-level overview of how you can create this chart:

1. First, you need to prepare your data. You'll need to have a dataset that contains the following information:
	* Source node (ABC suppliers)
	* Target node (DEF suppliers)
	* Value (the amount of money being transferred)
	* Reduction value (the amount of money being "cut" or reduced)
2. Next, you'll need to create a Sankey chart using Sankey.js. You can do this by creating a SVG element and adding the Sankey chart to it.
3. To visualize the reduction in the Sankey chart, you can use a combination of lines and arrows. You can create a line that connects the source node to the target node, and then add an arrow to the line to indicate the direction of the flow. You can also use a different color or thickness for the line to indicate the reduction in the value.
4. To make the chart more readable, you can add to labels the nodes and the links. You can use the `text` element to add labels to the nodes, and the `title` attribute to add a tooltip to the links.
5. Finally, you can customize the appearance of the chart by using CSS styles. You can use the `style` attribute to set the color, thickness, and other properties of the lines and nodes.

Here's sample some code to get you started:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Sankey Chart</title>
  <style>
    /* Add some basic styling to the chart */
    .node {
      shape: circle;
      fill: #ccc;
      stroke: #333;
      stroke-width: 2px;
    }
    .link {
      stroke: #666;
      stroke-width: 2px;
    }
    .reduced-link {
      stroke-dasharray: 5, 5;
      stroke: #999;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <svg width="800" height="600"></svg>
  <script src="https://d3js.org/d3.v7.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sankey.js@1.0.0/dist/sankey.min.js"></script>
  <script>
    // Load the data
    const data = [
      { source: "ABC1", target: "DEF1", value: 100, reduction: 20 },
      { source: "ABC2", target: "DEF2", value: 200, reduction: 30 },
      { source: "ABC3", target: "DEF3", value: 300, reduction: 40 },
    ];

    // Create the Sankey chart
    const sankey = d3.sankey()
      .nodeWidth(20)
      .nodePadding(10)
      .size([800, 600])
      .nodes(data.map((d) => ({ id: d.source, name: d.source })))
      .links(data.map((d) => ({
        source: d.source,
        target: d.target,
        value: d.value,
        reduction: d.reduction,
      })));

    // Create the SVG element
    const svg = d3.select("svg");

    // Add the Sankey chart to the SVG
    svg.append("g")
      .attr("transform", "translate(0, 0)")
      .call(sankey);

    // Add labels to the nodes
    svg.selectAll(".node")
      .append("text")
      .attr("x", 0)
      .attr("y", 0)
      .text((d) => d.name);

    // Add labels to the links
    svg.selectAll(".link")
      .append("title")
      .text((d) => `Value: ${d.value}, Reduction: ${d.reduction}`);

    // Add the reduced links
    svg.selectAll(".link")
      .filter((d) => d.reduction > 0)
      .classed("reduced-link", true);
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```
